INT. THORNQUIST DRESSMAKER'S SHOP - LONDON - DAY (1893)

Bolts of silk, a cutting table, a brass dummy in half-pinned lilac. Rain at the window.

AGNES THORNQUIST, 40s, pins in her mouth, kneels to a hem. DELPHINE ARCHAMBAULT, 20s, in her slip, stands on a footstool, arms out. Her eyes avoid the mirror.

COLONEL ASHBY-GREY, 60s, enters in a damp greatcoat. YUSUF HADDAD, 30s, tailor's apprentice, follows with a tape over his shoulder.

ASHBY-GREY
Miss Archambault. Your father sends his regards.

DELPHINE
How kind.

He sets his hat on the cutting table. Agnes takes the pins from her mouth.

AGNES
Colonel. We weren't expecting you.

ASHBY-GREY
One doesn't schedule a wedding fitting.

AGNES
It's not yet a wedding.

ASHBY-GREY
It will be Saturday.

Delphine's arm drops an inch. Agnes taps it back up.

AGNES
Steady, dear.

Yusuf unrolls the tape. Measures the waist. Says nothing. His fingers tremble.

ASHBY-GREY
Tighter at the bodice. The family insists.

AGNES
She'll not breathe.

ASHBY-GREY
She'll not need to.

A silence. Rain hits the glass.

Delphine looks into the mirror for the first time. At herself. At Yusuf behind her.

DELPHINE
Mr. Haddad. Is the silk imported?

YUSUF
From Damascus, miss.

DELPHINE
How far is that?

YUSUF
Very far.

DELPHINE
How long would it take to go?

Ashby-Grey turns. His eyes narrow.

ASHBY-GREY
Miss Archambault.

DELPHINE
Merely curious.

Agnes rises, sets her pins down, and smooths the bodice. Her thumb brushes Delphine's wrist. A pulse passes between them. Quick, quiet.

AGNES
(low)
I'll let out the seam tonight.

ASHBY-GREY
Pardon?

AGNES
I said I'll take in the seam tonight.

Ashby-Grey studies her. Then Yusuf. Then the door.

ASHBY-GREY
Saturday. Ten sharp.

He takes his hat and leaves. The bell tinkles. The latch clicks.

Nobody moves. Then Delphine steps down off the stool, slow, as if it were a scaffold.
